[03:39.00]M: Good evening and welcome to Physics Today.
[03:41.98]M: Here we interview some of the greatest minds in physics as they help us to understand some of the most complicated theories.
[03:49.64]M: Today, I'm very pleased to welcome Dr. Melissa Phillips, professor of theoretical physics.
[03:56.40]M: She's here to tell us a little about what it is she studies.
[04:00.66]M: Dr. Phillips, you seem to study everything.
[04:03.64]W: I guess that would be fair to say I spent most of my time studying the Big Bang theory and where our universe came from.
[04:11.44]M: Can you tell us a little about that?
[04:12.94]W: Well, I'm very interested in why the universe exists at all.
[04:17.52]W: That may sound odd, but the fact is at the moment of the Big Bang, both matter and anti-matter were created for a short time, and I mean just a fraction of a second.
[04:28.76]W: The whole universe was a super-hot soup of radiation filled with these particles.
[04:34.90]W: So what's baffled scientists for so long is "why is there a universe at all?"
[04:41.16]M: That's because matter and anti-matter are basically opposites of each other.
[04:45.82]M: They are exactly alike except that they have opposite electrical charges.
[04:50.58]M: So when they collide, they destroy each other?
[04:53.50]W: Exactly.
[04:54.72]W: So during the first few moments of the Big Bang, the universe was extremely hot and very small.
[05:01.78]W: Matter and the now more exotic anti-matter would have had little space to avoid each other.
[05:08.02]W: This means that they should have totally wiped each other out, leaving the universe completely barren.
[05:14.32]M: But a recent study seems to point to the fact that when matter and anti-matter were first created, there were slightly more particles of matter, which allowed the universe we all live in to form?
[05:25.36]W: Exactly.
[05:26.48]W: Because there was slightly more matter, the collisions quickly depleted all the anti-matter and left just enough matter to create stars, planets and eventually us.
