[07:27.34]In this week's edition of special series on Bizarre Medical Conditions, there is a report of the case of Michelle Myers.
[07:36.58]Myers is an American woman who woke up one day speaking with a British accent, even though she's lived in the United States all her life.
[07:45.92]In 2015, Myers went to bed with a terrible headache.
[07:50.86]She woke up sounding like someone from England.
[07:54.04]Her British accent has remained for the past two years.
[07:58.20]Previously, Myers had woken up speaking in Irish and Australian accents.
[08:04.14]However, on both of those occasions, the accents lasted for only a week.
[08:10.16]Myers has been diagnosed with Foreign Accent Syndrome.
[08:14.00]It's a disorder in which a person experiences a sudden change to their speech so that they sound like they're speaking in a foreign accent.
[08:22.38]The condition is most often caused by a stroke or traumatic brain injury.
[08:27.48]Although people with the syndrome have intelligible speech, their manner of speaking is altered in terms of timing and tongue placement, which may distort their pronunciation.
[08:38.18]The result is that they may sound foreign when speaking their native language.
[08:42.92]It's not clear whether Myers has experienced a stroke or other brain damage, but she also has a separate medical condition, which can result in loose joints, easily bruised skin and other problems.
[08:57.42]Foreign Accent Syndrome is rare, with only about 60 cases reported within the past century.
[09:04.10]However, a different American woman reportedly spoke with the Russian accent in 2010 after she fell down the stairs and hit her head.
[09:21.42]Q9. What happened to Michelle Myers one day?
[09:39.14]Q10. What does the passage say about Foreign Accent Syndrome?
