[13:56.26]Today I'm going to talk about a very special kind of person.
[14:00.48]Psychologists call them "masters of deception," those rare individuals with a natural ability to tell with complete confidence when someone is telling a lie.
[14:12.52]For decades, researchers and law enforcement agencies have tried to build a machine that will do the same thing.
[14:20.26]Now a company in Massachusetts says that by using magnetic brain scans they can determine with 97% accuracy whether someone is telling the truth.
[14:32.20]They hope that the technology will be cleared for use in American courts by early next year.
[14:38.30]But is this really the ultimate tool for you, the lawyers of tomorrow?
[14:43.92]You'll not find many brain scientists celebrating this breakthrough.
[14:47.88]The company might be very optimistic, but the ability of their machine to detect deception has not provided credible proof.
[14:56.70]That's because the technology has not been properly tested in real-world situations.
[15:02.48]In life, there are different kinds of lies and diverse context in which they're told.
[15:08.78]These differences may elicit different brain responses.
[15:13.20]Does their hypothesis behind the test apply in every case?
[15:17.76]We don't know the answer, because studies done on how reliable this machine is have not yet been duplicated.
[15:25.06]Much more research is badly needed.
[15:28.58]Whether the technology is eventually deemed reliable enough for the courts will ultimately be decided by the judges.
[15:36.34]Let's hope they're wise enough not to be fooled by a machine that claims to determine truthfulness at the flick of a switch.
[15:43.54]They should also be sceptical of the growing tendency to try to reduce all human traits and actions to the level of brain activity.
[15:52.60]Often, they do not map that easily.
[15:56.10]Moreover, understanding the brain is not the same as understanding the mind.
[16:01.80]Some researchers have suggested that thoughts cannot properly be seen as purely "internal."
[16:08.36]Instead, thoughts make sense only in reference to the individual's external world.
[16:14.90]So while there may be insights to be gained from matching behavior to brain activity, those insights will not necessarily lead to justice in a court of law.
[16:27.20]Problems surround the use of machines to spot deception, at least until it has been rigorously tested.
[16:34.82]A high-tech test that can tell when a person is not telling the truth sounds too good to be true.
[16:42.22]And when something sounds too good to be true, it usually is.
[16:54.86]Q16. What have researchers and law enforcement agencies tried to do?
[17:14.88]Q17. How do many brain scientists respond to the Massachusetts company's so-called technological breakthrough?
[17:37.58]Q18. What does the speaker think of using a high-tech test to determine whether a person is telling the truth?
[17:59.02]Last week I attended a research workshop on an island in the South Pacific.
[18:05.46]Thirty people were present and all except me came from the island, called Makelua, in the nation of Vanuatu.
[18:13.64]They live in 16 different communities and speak 16 distinct languages.
[18:19.06]In many cases, you could stand at the edge of one village and see the outskirts of the next community.
[18:25.66]Yet the residents of each village speak a completely different language.
[18:30.32]According to recent work by my colleagues at the Max Planck Institute for the Science of Human History, this island, just 100 kilometers long and 20 kilometers wide, is home to speakers of perhaps 40 different indigenous languages.
[18:47.46]Why so many?
[18:49.06]We could ask the same question of the entire globe.
[18:53.04]People don't speak one universal language, or even a handful.
[18:56.92]Instead, today our species collectively speaks over 7,000 distinct languages, and these languages are not spread randomly across the planet.
[19:08.66]For example, far more languages are found in tropical regions than in the mild zones.
[19:15.30]The tropical island of New Guinea is home to over 900 languages.
[19:20.24]Russia, 20 times larger, has 105 indigenous languages.
[19:25.40]Even within the tropics, language diversity varies widely.
[19:30.94]For example, the 250,000 people who live on Vanuatu's 80 islands speak 110 different languages, but in Bangladesh, a population 600 times greater speaks only 41 languages.
[19:48.04]How come humans speak so many languages?
[19:50.46]And why are they so unevenly spread across the planet?
[19:55.44]As it turns out, we have few clear answers to these fundamental questions about how humanity communicates.
[20:03.24]Most people can easily brainstorm possible answers to these intriguing questions.
[20:09.26]They hypothesize that language diversity must be about history, cultural differences, mountains or oceans dividing populations.
[20:18.50]But when our diverse team of researchers from six different disciplines and eight different countries began to review what was known, we were shocked that only a dozen previous studies had been done, including one we ourselves completed on language diversity in the Pacific.
[20:37.86]These prior efforts all examined the degree to which different environmental, social and geographic variables correlated with the number of languages found in a given location.
[20:50.56]The results varied a lot from one study to another, and no clear patterns emerged.
[20:56.44]The studies also ran up against many methodological challenges, the biggest of which centered on the old statistical saying—correlation does not equal causation.
